course: dictionary
lesson: 7311
title: Nouns and the word pizza
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: A noun is a naming word. It names a person, place or thing. «Pizza» is a noun, and you can count it: one pizza, two pizzas. To make more than one, you add s: pizzas.
explain: Context tells you what a word is doing. In «At Dad's, Ruaridh gets pizza and stays up till ten», pizza is a treat for a special night. Look at the words around a noun to find clues about what it is.
explain: Pizza is also a maths word. «If the slices do not fill the last pizza, you still need one more whole pizza» shows that a pizza is shared into equal slices, and you must count them carefully.
fact: A noun is a naming word for a person, place or thing.
fact: «Pizza» means a flat round bread baked with tomato, cheese and other toppings on top.
fact: A pizza comes from Italy.
fact: The plural of «pizza» is «pizzas».
fact: A pizza cut into equal slices can show fractions.
exercise: What is a noun? | A word that joins two sentences | An action word, like run or jump | A word that describes a noun, like big | *A naming word for a person, place or thing
exercise: What is the plural of pizza? | *pizzas | pizza's | pizzies | pizzaes
exercise: «Building with bricks is two kinds of fun.» Find and copy one noun that names a thing. | *bricks | inventing | following | kinds
test: Which of these is a pizza topping? | Bricks | *Mushrooms | Chocolate sauce | Pencils
test: A pizza is cut into 8 equal slices. What fraction is 2 slices? | 1/3 | 1/2 | *1/4 | 1/8
exam: 9 children each eat 2 slices, and each pizza has 8 slices. How many pizzas must you order? | 2 | 1 | *3 | 4
